var articles = {
// ===== 科学科技 =====
science: {title:"How Do Batteries Store Energy?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Batteries convert chemical energy into electrical energy. Inside every battery are three essential parts: a positive electrode called the cathode, a negative electrode called the anode, and a chemical medium called the electrolyte that sits between them. When you connect a battery to a device, a chemical reaction begins at the anode. Electrons are released and flow through the external circuit, powering your device, before returning to the cathode. This flow of electrons is what we call electric current. The specific chemicals used determine the battery's voltage and capacity. Alkaline batteries use zinc and manganese dioxide. Lithium-ion batteries, which power most smartphones and laptops, use lithium compounds that can store more energy in less space. Rechargeable batteries work because the chemical reaction is reversible. When you plug in a charger, electric current forces the reaction to run backward, restoring the original chemicals. The lithium-ion battery was commercialized by Sony in 1991 and transformed portable electronics. Today, researchers are developing solid-state batteries that promise even higher energy density, faster charging, and improved safety."},
"science-2":{title:"Why Is the Sky Blue?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Sunlight appears white, but it is actually a mixture of all colors. When this light enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules and tiny particles. Different colors have different wavelengths. Blue light has a much shorter wavelength than red light, and shorter wavelengths are scattered far more by atmospheric particles. This phenomenon is called Rayleigh scattering, after the British physicist Lord Rayleigh who explained it mathematically in the 1870s. The amount of scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength, meaning blue light is scattered about ten times more than red light. When you look at the sky away from the sun, you see this scattered blue light. At sunrise and sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere to reach your eyes. Most of the blue light has been scattered away, leaving the longer red and orange wavelengths. This is why the sky near the horizon glows with warm colors at dawn and dusk. On the Moon, which has no atmosphere, the sky is always black."},
"science-3":{title:"The Search for Dark Matter",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Everything we can see accounts for only about five percent of the universe. Roughly 27 percent is dark matter, and 68 percent is dark energy. Dark matter does not emit, absorb, or reflect light, making it completely invisible. We know it exists because of its gravitational effects. Galaxies rotate so fast that, based on visible mass alone, they should fly apart. Something invisible holds them together. That something is dark matter. It also bends light from distant galaxies through gravitational lensing. Scientists have built detectors deep underground, shielded from cosmic rays, hoping to catch a dark matter particle. The leading candidates are WIMPs and axions. Despite decades of searching, no direct detection has been confirmed. Solving this mystery would fundamentally reshape physics."},
"science-4":{title:"How Does Wi-Fi Actually Work?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Wi-Fi uses radio waves to transmit data between your device and a router. These waves operate at frequencies of 2.4 or 5 gigahertz, similar to microwave ovens but at much lower power. When you send an email or stream a video, your device converts the data into a radio signal. The router receives this signal, decodes it, and forwards it to the internet through a physical cable. The whole process takes milliseconds. Wi-Fi is based on the IEEE 802.11 family of standards, first released in 1997. Each generation has become faster. Wi-Fi 6, the latest widely adopted version, can handle many devices simultaneously without congestion. The name Wi-Fi does not actually stand for anything. It was chosen by a branding company because it sounded catchy. Despite rumors, it is not short for wireless fidelity. The biggest limitation of Wi-Fi is range. Walls, furniture, and distance all weaken the signal. Mesh networks, which use multiple router nodes, have become popular for covering larger homes with reliable Wi-Fi."},
"science-5":{title:"What Causes Earthquakes?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The Earth's surface is broken into about 15 massive pieces called tectonic plates. These plates float on a layer of hot, slowly moving rock beneath them. They move at about the same speed your fingernails grow. When two plates push against each other at a fault line, friction locks them in place while pressure builds up over decades or centuries. Eventually, the rocks break and release all that stored energy as seismic waves, shaking the ground. This is an earthquake. Most occur along plate boundaries. The Pacific Ring of Fire, encircling the Pacific Ocean, is the most seismically active region on Earth. The Richter scale, developed in 1935, was the standard way to measure earthquakes. Today, scientists more commonly use the moment magnitude scale, which is more accurate for large earthquakes. Each whole number increase represents about 32 times more energy released. The largest recorded earthquake, a magnitude 9.5 in Chile in 1960, released energy equivalent to about 20,000 Hiroshima bombs."},
"science-6":{title:"The Mystery of Black Holes",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so intense that nothing, not even light, can escape. Black holes form when very massive stars exhaust their nuclear fuel and collapse under their own gravity. The boundary around a black hole is called the event horizon. Once anything crosses this boundary, it can never return. At the center lies the singularity, where matter is crushed to infinite density and the known laws of physics break down. In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope collaboration captured the first-ever image of a black hole, showing the shadow of the supermassive black hole at the center of galaxy M87, 55 million light-years away. The image confirmed predictions made by Einstein's theory of general relativity over a century ago. Black holes are not cosmic vacuum cleaners. If the Sun were replaced by a black hole of the same mass, Earth's orbit would not change. The danger comes only when you get very close."},
"science-7":{title:"How Do Vaccines Protect Us?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A vaccine trains your immune system to recognize and fight a pathogen without causing the disease itself. It contains a weakened or inactivated form of the germ, or just a piece of it, like a protein from its surface. When injected, your immune system treats it as a threat and produces antibodies. These antibodies remain in your body, sometimes for life. If you later encounter the real pathogen, your immune system recognizes it and mounts a rapid defense before you get sick. The first vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner in 1796. He noticed that milkmaids who caught cowpox, a mild disease, seemed immune to the deadly smallpox. He tested his theory by inoculating a boy with cowpox and later exposing him to smallpox. The boy did not get sick. This experiment laid the foundation for immunology. Vaccines have since eradicated smallpox and dramatically reduced polio, measles, and many other diseases. The rapid development of mRNA vaccines during the COVID-19 pandemic represented a new chapter in vaccine technology."},
"science-8":{title:"The Internet: A Brief History",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The internet began in the late 1960s as ARPANET, a project funded by the U.S. Department of Defense. The goal was to create a communication network that could survive a nuclear attack by routing messages around damaged nodes. In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee, a British scientist at CERN, invented the World Wide Web. Before the Web, using the internet required typing arcane commands. Berners-Lee created three fundamental technologies: HTML for writing web pages, HTTP for transmitting them, and URLs for addressing them. The first web browser, Mosaic, was released in 1993 and made the Web accessible to ordinary people. By 2000, hundreds of millions were online. The smartphone era, beginning with the iPhone in 2007, put the internet in people's pockets. Today, over five billion people use the internet. It has reshaped commerce, education, politics, and social interaction. Yet the digital divide remains: roughly a third of humanity still lacks reliable internet access."},
"science-9":{title:"What Are Stars Made Of?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Stars are giant balls of mostly hydrogen and helium gas, held together by their own gravity. In a star's core, the temperature and pressure are so intense that hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium, releasing enormous energy as light and heat. This process, nuclear fusion, has powered our Sun for about 4.6 billion years. It will continue for another 5 billion years before the Sun exhausts its hydrogen fuel. Stars come in a wide range of sizes, temperatures, and colors. Blue stars are the hottest, with surface temperatures exceeding 30,000 degrees Celsius. Red stars are the coolest, at around 3,000 degrees. When a star like our Sun runs out of fuel, it swells into a red giant and then sheds its outer layers, leaving behind a dense white dwarf. More massive stars end their lives in spectacular supernova explosions, sometimes leaving behind neutron stars or black holes. The elements that make up our bodies — carbon, oxygen, iron — were forged in the cores of ancient stars and scattered across space when they died."},
"science-10":{title:"How Does GPS Find Your Location?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The Global Positioning System is a constellation of about 30 satellites orbiting Earth at an altitude of 20,000 kilometers. Each satellite continuously broadcasts a radio signal containing its exact position and the precise time. Your phone listens for these signals. By measuring how long each signal took to arrive, your phone calculates its distance from each satellite. With signals from at least four satellites, it can determine your three-dimensional position through trilateration. The precision is remarkable. GPS can locate you within a few meters. The system relies on atomic clocks aboard each satellite, which are accurate to within a few billionths of a second. GPS was developed by the U.S. military and became fully operational in 1995. It was opened to civilian use after a Korean Airlines flight was shot down in 1983 for straying into Soviet airspace, highlighting the need for accurate global navigation. Today, GPS is integrated into everything from farming equipment to earthquake monitoring systems."},
"science-11":{title:"The Amazing World of DNA",source:"Wikipedia",text:"DNA is the instruction manual for all known life. The name stands for deoxyribonucleic acid, and its structure is a double helix: two strands twisted around each other like a spiral staircase. The rungs of the staircase are made of four chemical bases: adenine pairs with thymine, and guanine pairs with cytosine. The order of these bases encodes genetic information, much like letters form words and sentences. The human genome contains about three billion base pairs. If you stretched out the DNA from a single human cell, it would be about two meters long. In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick, building on the work of Rosalind Franklin, discovered the double helix structure. Their discovery revolutionized biology. Today, DNA sequencing costs have dropped dramatically. What once cost billions now costs under a thousand dollars. This has enabled personalized medicine, where treatments are tailored to a patient's genetic profile, and forensic science, where DNA evidence can solve decades-old crimes."},
"science-12":{title:"How Do Airplanes Stay in the Air?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Four forces act on an airplane in flight: lift pushes it up, weight pulls it down, thrust pushes it forward, and drag pulls it back. Lift is generated by the wings. A wing is shaped so that air flows faster over its curved top than its flatter bottom. According to Bernoulli's principle, faster-moving air exerts less pressure. The higher pressure below the wing pushes upward. Thrust comes from the engines. In jet engines, air is compressed, mixed with fuel, ignited, and expelled at high speed. Newton's third law — every action has an equal and opposite reaction — pushes the plane forward. Drag is air resistance, which engineers minimize through streamlined design. Weight is simply gravity acting on the plane's mass. For level flight, lift must equal weight and thrust must equal drag. Pilots adjust control surfaces — flaps, ailerons, and rudders — to change these forces and maneuver the aircraft."},
"science-13":{title:"What Is Artificial Intelligence?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Artificial intelligence refers to machines performing tasks that normally require human intelligence: recognizing patterns, understanding language, making decisions. Modern AI is built on machine learning. Instead of being explicitly programmed with rules, a machine learning system is trained on vast amounts of data. It finds statistical patterns that humans might miss or could never process manually. Deep learning, a more advanced form, uses artificial neural networks loosely inspired by the brain's structure. These networks have many layers, each extracting increasingly abstract features from the input data. AI powers search engines, voice assistants, recommendation algorithms, and medical diagnosis tools. It can translate languages, generate images from text descriptions, and even write computer code. But current AI has fundamental limitations. It does not truly understand what it processes. It can produce confident-sounding falsehoods, reflect biases in its training data, and fail in unpredictable ways. As AI systems become more powerful, society faces difficult questions about jobs, privacy, accountability, and the nature of intelligence itself."},
"science-14":{title:"The Speed of Light",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Light travels at 299,792,458 meters per second in a vacuum. That is fast enough to circle the Earth seven and a half times in one second. Nothing with mass can reach this speed. According to Einstein's special relativity, as an object approaches the speed of light, its mass increases toward infinity and time slows down relative to outside observers. This is not just theory. It has been experimentally confirmed by comparing atomic clocks on airplanes with those on the ground. Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth. Light from the nearest star beyond the Sun takes 4.3 years. When you look at the Andromeda Galaxy, you are seeing light that left it 2.5 million years ago, before modern humans existed. The speed of light is not just about light. It is the maximum speed at which any information or causal influence can travel through space. It is woven into the fabric of the universe and appears in equations across physics, from electromagnetism to gravity."},
"science-15":{title:"How Do 3D Printers Work?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A 3D printer builds objects layer by layer, a process called additive manufacturing. It starts with a digital model, which software slices into hundreds of thin horizontal layers. The printer then deposits or solidifies material one layer at a time. The most common home type, FDM, melts plastic filament through a heated nozzle. Industrial printers work with metals, ceramics, and even living cells. Applications range from prototyping products to custom medical implants and experimental organ printing."},
"science-16":{title:"How Do Touchscreens Work?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Modern smartphones use capacitive touchscreens. The screen is coated with a transparent conductor that holds a uniform electrostatic field. Your finger carries a tiny electrical charge. When you touch the screen, it draws a small current from each corner of the panel. The processor measures these currents and calculates the exact touch position. This all happens in about a hundredth of a second. Multi-touch capability, which enables pinch-to-zoom and two-finger gestures, works by tracking multiple contact points simultaneously. Older resistive touchscreens used pressure instead of electricity and could not support multi-touch. They were common on ATMs and early PDAs. The first capacitive touchscreen was developed in 1965 at the Royal Radar Establishment in England, decades before smartphones made the technology ubiquitous."},
"science-17":{title:"What Is Quantum Computing?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Quantum computers harness the strange rules of quantum mechanics to solve certain problems exponentially faster than classical computers. While regular computers use bits that are either 0 or 1, quantum computers use qubits that can exist in a superposition of both states simultaneously. Another quantum property, entanglement, allows qubits to be correlated such that measuring one instantly determines the state of another, regardless of distance. These properties enable quantum algorithms to explore many possible solutions at once. In 2019, Google claimed quantum supremacy: its 53-qubit processor solved a specific problem in 200 seconds that would take the fastest classical supercomputer about 10,000 years. However, the problem was contrived and had no practical use. The real promise lies in simulating molecules for drug discovery, optimizing supply chains, and breaking certain encryption schemes. The main challenge is decoherence: qubits are extremely fragile and lose their quantum properties at the slightest disturbance. Building a fault-tolerant, general-purpose quantum computer remains one of the hardest engineering challenges of our time."},
"science-18":{title:"The Science of Rainbows",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A rainbow forms when sunlight enters a raindrop, bends, reflects off the back, and bends again as it exits. This double refraction, called dispersion, separates white light into its component colors because each color bends by a slightly different amount. Violet bends the most and red the least. A primary rainbow always appears at an angle of about 42 degrees from the direction opposite the sun, with red on the outside and violet on the inside. A secondary, fainter rainbow sometimes appears outside the primary one with reversed colors, caused by light reflecting twice inside the drop. The dark region between them is Alexander's band. You cannot reach the end of a rainbow because it is an optical effect that moves with your perspective."},
"science-19":{title:"How Your Voice Travels Through a Phone",source:"Wikipedia",text:"When you speak into a phone, a microphone converts your voice's sound waves into electrical signals. An analog-to-digital converter samples these signals thousands of times per second and converts them into a stream of binary data. This data is compressed, broken into packets, and transmitted through fiber optic cables, cell towers, or satellites. Each packet may take a different route across the network. At the receiving end, the packets are reassembled, converted back to analog signals, and sent to a speaker that vibrates to recreate the original sound. The entire process takes less than a hundred milliseconds, making conversations feel instantaneous even when spanning continents."},
"science-20":{title:"The Search for Exoplanets",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Before 1992, we did not know if planets existed around other stars. Today, astronomers have confirmed more than 5,000 exoplanets. Most are detected through two indirect methods. The transit method watches for a tiny dip in a star's brightness when a planet passes in front of it. The radial velocity method detects the slight wobble of a star caused by an orbiting planet's gravity. The Kepler Space Telescope, launched in 2009, discovered thousands of exoplanets using the transit method. Some are rocky like Earth. Others are gas giants many times Jupiter's size. A precious few orbit in the habitable zone, where temperatures could permit liquid water. The James Webb Space Telescope can now analyze the atmospheres of some exoplanets, searching for gases like oxygen and methane that might indicate biological activity."},
"science-21":{title:"What Makes Popcorn Pop?",source:"Scientific American",text:"Each popcorn kernel contains a small amount of water trapped inside a hard, moisture-proof shell called the pericarp. When heated, the water turns to steam and the pressure inside builds to about nine times atmospheric pressure. At around 180 degrees Celsius, the shell suddenly ruptures. The superheated starch inside expands explosively, turning the kernel inside out. The white fluffy material is solidified starch foam. High-speed cameras reveal the kernel actually performs a little jump as it pops, propelled by the escaping steam. The ideal moisture content for popping is about 14 percent. Too dry and the kernel lacks the explosive force. Too moist and the shell cracks prematurely, yielding a dense, chewy result rather than the light, crispy snack we know."},
"science-22":{title:"The Mystery of Antimatter",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Every particle of matter has a corresponding antimatter particle with the same mass but opposite charge. The electron's antiparticle is the positron. When matter and antimatter meet, they annihilate in a burst of pure energy according to Einstein's equation E=mc². The Big Bang should have created equal amounts of both. Yet the observable universe is overwhelmingly made of matter. Where the antimatter went is one of physics' deepest mysteries. Experiments at CERN study subtle asymmetries called CP violation that might explain the imbalance. Antimatter also has practical uses. PET scans use positrons to detect metabolic activity in the body, helping diagnose cancer and neurological conditions."},
"science-23":{title:"How Does a Microwave Heat Food?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A microwave oven generates electromagnetic waves at about 2.45 gigahertz. At this frequency, the waves cause polarized water molecules in food to rotate rapidly back and forth, billions of times per second. The friction between these vibrating molecules generates heat throughout the food, not just at the surface. This is why microwaves heat food much faster than conventional ovens. Metal reflects microwaves, which is why metal containers should not be used. The waves bounce off the metal walls of the oven and penetrate the food from multiple angles. A turntable rotates the food to prevent hot and cold spots. The microwave was discovered accidentally in 1945 when engineer Percy Spencer noticed a chocolate bar in his pocket melted while he worked near an active radar set."},
"science-24":{title:"The Future of Space Travel",source:"NASA and SpaceX",text:"For decades, only governments could afford human spaceflight. Today, private companies are transforming access to space. SpaceX's Falcon 9 rocket can land and be reused, cutting launch costs dramatically. Its Starship, the largest rocket ever built, is designed to carry up to a hundred people to Mars. NASA's Artemis program aims to return humans to the Moon and establish a permanent base as a stepping stone for deeper space exploration. The challenges remain formidable. Astronauts face radiation exposure, bone density loss from microgravity, and the psychological strain of isolation. Engineers are developing nuclear thermal rockets that could cut travel time to Mars from months to weeks. For the first time, a multiplanetary future seems within reach."},
"science-25":{title:"What Causes the Seasons?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A common misconception is that summer is warmer because Earth is closer to the Sun. In reality, the seasons are caused by Earth's 23.5-degree axial tilt. When the Northern Hemisphere tilts toward the Sun, sunlight strikes it more directly and days are longer, producing summer. The Southern Hemisphere experiences winter at the same time because it is tilted away. Six months later, the situation reverses. The distance between Earth and Sun varies by about 5 million kilometers over the year but has minimal effect on temperature compared to the tilt-driven angle of sunlight."},

// ===== 健康 =====
health: {title:"The Science of Sleep",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Sleep is far more than rest. While you sleep, your brain cycles through non-REM and REM stages in roughly 90-minute loops. During deep non-REM sleep, your body repairs tissues, builds bone and muscle, and strengthens the immune system. Your brain clears out metabolic waste products, including beta-amyloid, a protein associated with Alzheimer's disease. During REM sleep, named for the rapid eye movements that occur, most dreaming takes place. The brain consolidates memories, processes emotions, and strengthens neural connections formed during the day. Adults need 7 to 9 hours of sleep per night. Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours. Chronic sleep deprivation is linked to heart disease, diabetes, depression, and impaired cognitive function. To improve sleep, experts recommend a consistent bedtime, a cool and dark bedroom, and avoiding screens for at least an hour before sleep. The blue light from screens suppresses melatonin, the hormone that signals your body it is time to rest."},
"health-2":{title:"How Your Gut Affects Your Mood",source:"Harvard Medical School",text:"Inside your digestive system live trillions of bacteria, collectively called the gut microbiome. These microorganisms weigh about as much as your brain. They help digest food and produce essential vitamins, but their influence extends much further. The gut and brain are connected by the vagus nerve, forming what scientists call the gut-brain axis. Gut bacteria produce neurotransmitters including serotonin, dopamine, and GABA. Remarkably, about 90 percent of the body's serotonin, a key mood regulator, is made in the gut. Research has found that people with diverse gut bacteria tend to have lower rates of anxiety and depression. A diet rich in fiber, vegetables, and fermented foods like yogurt and kimchi supports a healthy microbiome. While probiotic supplements are popular, scientists caution that the field is still young. The best current advice is to eat a varied, plant-rich diet."},
"health-3":{title:"Why Exercise Makes You Happier",source:"Mayo Clinic",text:"Physical activity benefits the brain as much as the body. When you exercise, your body releases endorphins, natural chemicals that reduce pain perception and produce feelings of euphoria often called the runner's high. Exercise also stimulates the production of BDNF, a protein that supports the growth and survival of neurons. It is especially active in the hippocampus, a brain region critical for memory that shrinks with age and depression. Studies have found that regular aerobic exercise can be as effective as antidepressant medication for mild to moderate depression. You do not need intense workouts. A 30-minute walk three times a week measurably improves mood and cognitive function. The key is consistency, not intensity."},
"health-4":{title:"The Dangers of Sitting Too Much",source:"WHO",text:"The average office worker spends about 10 hours a day sitting. Prolonged sitting slows metabolism, reduces the activity of enzymes that break down fat, and makes muscles less responsive to insulin. Over years, this raises the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and some cancers. Even regular exercise does not fully offset the damage of extended sitting. The solution is simple: interrupt sitting time. Getting up and moving for just two minutes every hour improves metabolic markers. Standing desks help, but the real benefit comes from movement, not just standing still. The human body evolved for near-constant low-level activity, not for sitting in chairs."},
"health-5":{title:"How the Immune System Fights Disease",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Your immune system is a distributed defense network. White blood cells patrol the bloodstream and tissues, identifying and destroying pathogens. Some, called macrophages, engulf invaders whole. Others, called B cells, produce antibodies that tag pathogens for destruction. T cells can directly kill infected cells. After an infection, memory cells remain in the body for years, sometimes for life, enabling a rapid response if the same pathogen returns. This is the basis of vaccination. The immune system is remarkably effective but can malfunction. Allergies occur when it overreacts to harmless substances. Autoimmune diseases result when it mistakenly attacks the body's own tissues. Keeping the immune system healthy requires adequate sleep, regular exercise, good nutrition, and managing stress."},
"health-6":{title:"What Happens When You Drink Water",source:"Medical research",text:"Water makes up about 60 percent of adult body weight. Every system depends on it. Water regulates body temperature through sweating, lubricates joints, protects the spinal cord, and enables kidneys to filter waste. Even mild dehydration, as little as one to two percent of body weight lost as water, impairs concentration, memory, and physical performance. The sensation of thirst means you are already slightly dehydrated. Most adults need about 2 to 3 liters of fluids per day, though needs vary with climate and activity. Water from food, especially fruits and vegetables, contributes about 20 percent of intake. A simple hydration check: pale straw-colored urine indicates good hydration, while dark yellow signals you need more water."},
"health-7":{title:"The Benefits of Reading for Mental Health",source:"Neuroscience research",text:"Reading reduces stress measurably. One study found that just six minutes of reading lowered heart rate and muscle tension more effectively than listening to music or taking a walk. Regular readers maintain sharper cognitive function as they age and have lower risk of dementia. Reading fiction specifically builds empathy. Brain scans show that when you read about a character's experience, the same neural regions activate as if you were living through it yourself. This trains your ability to understand others' emotions. Deep reading, the sustained, undistracted immersion in a text, is qualitatively different from the quick scanning we do online. It builds the capacity for focus, reflection, and complex thought in an increasingly distracted world."},
"health-8":{title:"Understanding Nutrition Labels",source:"FDA",text:"Nutrition labels can be confusing, but a few simple principles help. First, check the serving size. A package that looks like one portion may contain two or three. Pay special attention to added sugars, distinct from natural sugars in fruit and milk. High added sugar intake is linked to obesity, diabetes, and heart disease. Look for fiber and protein, which slow digestion and keep you full. Most people consume far more sodium than needed, primarily from processed foods. As a rule of thumb, foods with shorter ingredient lists and recognizable ingredients tend to be less processed. The healthiest foods, like fresh produce, often have no label at all."},
"health-9":{title:"How Stress Affects Your Body",source:"APA",text:"Stress triggers the release of cortisol and adrenaline, hormones that prepare the body for immediate action. Heart rate increases, blood pressure rises, and energy stores are mobilized. This response is life-saving in short bursts. But when stress becomes chronic, the same hormones become toxic. Persistent high cortisol is linked to weight gain, high blood pressure, weakened immunity, and shrinkage of the hippocampus, a brain region essential for memory. Effective stress management is not about eliminating stress entirely. It is about recovery between stress episodes. Deep breathing, physical exercise, social connection, and spending time in nature all lower cortisol levels. Even a few minutes of deliberate slow breathing can shift the body from fight-or-flight mode to a calmer state."},
"health-10":{title:"The Truth About Vitamins",source:"NIH",text:"For most healthy people eating a balanced diet, vitamin supplements provide no proven benefit and may even cause harm in high doses. Whole foods contain fiber and thousands of phytochemicals that work together in ways isolated pills cannot replicate. However, specific groups do benefit from supplements: pregnant women need folic acid, exclusively breastfed infants need vitamin D, and vegans require vitamin B12. People with diagnosed deficiencies also need targeted supplementation. The supplement industry is lightly regulated in many countries. Products can be sold without proof of safety or effectiveness. Before taking supplements, consult a healthcare provider and remember that more is not better."},
"health-11":{title:"Why Breakfast Matters",source:"Harvard School of Public Health",text:"After an overnight fast, your brain needs glucose to function optimally. Studies show that eating breakfast improves memory, concentration, and mood, especially in children and adolescents. People who eat breakfast tend to make healthier food choices throughout the day and are less likely to overeat at lunch. However, the quality of breakfast matters greatly. A breakfast rich in protein and fiber from foods like eggs, oatmeal, or yogurt provides sustained energy. Sugary cereals and pastries cause a rapid blood sugar spike followed by a crash that leaves you tired and hungry. If you are not hungry in the morning, start with something small and build the habit gradually."},
"health-12":{title:"The Power of Meditation",source:"Scientific research",text:"Meditation is not mystical. It is mental training. Brain imaging studies show that regular meditation physically changes brain structure. It increases gray matter density in regions associated with learning, memory, and emotional regulation, while reducing the size of the amygdala, the brain's fear and stress center. Benefits appear with as little as 10 minutes of daily practice. There are many forms. Mindfulness meditation involves paying attention to the present moment without judgment. Focused attention meditation concentrates on a single object, often the breath. Loving-kindness meditation cultivates feelings of goodwill toward oneself and others. The common thread is training the mind to notice when it has wandered and gently bring it back, strengthening the neural circuits for attention and emotional control."},
"health-13":{title:"How Your Lungs Work",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Your two lungs contain about 2,400 kilometers of airways and roughly 300 million tiny air sacs called alveoli. The total surface area of these sacs is about the size of a tennis court, all folded into your chest. With each breath, air travels down your windpipe, through branching tubes called bronchi and bronchioles, and finally into the alveoli. Oxygen passes through the gossamer-thin walls of the alveoli into surrounding capillaries, while carbon dioxide moves in the opposite direction to be exhaled. This gas exchange happens in fractions of a second and repeats about 20,000 times every day. Breathing is controlled automatically by the brainstem but can be overridden voluntarily, a unique feature among vital functions. You cannot will your heart to stop, but you can hold your breath."},
"health-14":{title:"The Science of Allergies",source:"Wikipedia",text:"An allergy is the immune system mistaking a harmless substance for a threat. Common triggers include pollen, dust mites, pet dander, and certain foods. On first exposure, the body produces specific IgE antibodies that attach to mast cells. On subsequent exposure, these cells release histamine and other chemicals, causing the familiar symptoms of sneezing, itching, and swelling. Severe reactions, called anaphylaxis, can be life-threatening. Allergies are increasing globally. One leading explanation is the hygiene hypothesis: modern environments are so clean that developing immune systems, lacking real pathogens to fight, overreact to benign substances. Treatments range from antihistamines to immunotherapy, which gradually desensitizes the immune system through controlled exposure."},
"health-15":{title:"Why Do We Dream?",source:"Psychology research",text:"The function of dreams remains debated. The leading theory is that dreams help process emotions and consolidate memories. During REM sleep, the brain replays and reorganizes experiences, strengthening important connections and pruning irrelevant ones. Another theory suggests dreams simulate threats, allowing safe practice of responses. Most people dream about two hours nightly, though most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking."},
"health-16":{title:"The Importance of Vitamin D",source:"NIH",text:"Vitamin D is unique because your body produces it when sunlight hits your skin. It helps absorb calcium for strong bones. Deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults. Vitamin D also supports immune function. Many people, especially in northern latitudes or who stay indoors, are deficient. Fatty fish, egg yolks, and fortified milk provide modest amounts. Fifteen to twenty minutes of sun exposure a few times a week usually suffices. Blood tests can confirm levels, and supplements should only be taken if deficiency is diagnosed, as excess vitamin D can be toxic."},
"health-17":{title:"How Antibiotics Work",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop them from multiplying, but they are useless against viruses. The first, penicillin, was discovered accidentally by Alexander Fleming in 1928 when mold contaminated one of his culture dishes. Some antibiotics attack the bacterial cell wall. Others block protein production or DNA replication. Overuse in medicine and agriculture has driven the rise of antibiotic-resistant superbugs. To slow resistance, doctors prescribe antibiotics more cautiously and patients must complete the full course even after feeling better. New antibiotics are desperately needed, but pharmaceutical companies have little financial incentive to develop them because they are taken for short courses rather than chronically."},
"health-18":{title:"What Happens When You Have a Fever",source:"Medical research",text:"Fever is a defense, not a disease. When immune cells detect an infection, they release chemicals that signal the brain's hypothalamus to raise the body's temperature set point. The elevated temperature slows pathogen reproduction and speeds up immune responses. Most fevers are harmless and self-limiting. Very high fevers above 40 degrees Celsius can be dangerous, especially in young children. Fever-reducing medicines like paracetamol ease discomfort but may slightly prolong illness by interfering with the body's natural defense. Staying hydrated is essential because fever increases fluid loss."},
"health-19":{title:"The Gut-Brain Connection",source:"Harvard Medical School",text:"The gut and brain communicate bidirectionally through the vagus nerve. Gut bacteria produce neurotransmitters that influence mood and cognition. About 90 percent of serotonin is produced in the gut. An unbalanced microbiome is associated with depression, anxiety, and even neurodegenerative diseases. Diet strongly shapes the microbiome. Fiber-rich plant foods promote beneficial bacteria. Fermented foods introduce live cultures. While probiotic supplements are popular, the science is still developing. The most reliable way to support gut health is eating a diverse, plant-forward diet and minimizing ultra-processed foods."},
"health-20":{title:"Why Handwashing Works",source:"CDC",text:"Soap is remarkably effective against viruses and bacteria. Soap molecules have a water-loving head and a fat-loving tail. The tail burrows into the fatty membrane that surrounds many pathogens, prying it apart. The friction of rubbing loosens germs from skin, and rinsing flushes everything away. Alcohol-based sanitizers denature proteins but do not remove dirt and are less effective on visibly soiled hands. The WHO recommends washing for at least 20 seconds, roughly the time it takes to hum Happy Birthday twice. Handwashing with soap reduces diarrheal disease by about 40 percent and respiratory infections significantly. It is one of the cheapest and most effective public health interventions ever devised."},
"health-21":{title:"The Science of Stretching",source:"Sports medicine",text:"Stretching lengthens muscle fibers and the connective tissue around them. Muscles contain spindles that detect changes in length. Stretch too fast and the spindle triggers a reflexive contraction to protect the muscle, which is why slow, steady stretching is more effective than bouncing. Regular stretching improves flexibility mainly by training the nervous system to tolerate greater extension, not by physically lengthening muscles. Static stretching before exercise may slightly reduce power output. Dynamic stretches are preferred for warm-ups. Static stretching is best saved for after workouts, when muscles are warm and pliable."},
"health-22":{title:"How the Body Heals a Cut",source:"Medical research",text:"Within seconds of a cut, blood platelets aggregate at the wound to form a clot. White blood cells arrive to clear bacteria and debris. Fibroblasts then produce collagen, forming a scaffold for new tissue. New blood vessels grow into the area. Finally, the outer skin layer regenerates to close the wound. This process takes days to weeks. Contrary to old advice, wounds heal faster in a moist environment. Covering a wound with a bandage and keeping it clean speeds healing and reduces scarring. Deep or dirty wounds may require medical attention to prevent infection."},
"health-23":{title:"The Power of Music Therapy",source:"AMTA",text:"Music therapy uses listening, singing, and playing instruments for therapeutic goals. It reduces pain and anxiety in hospital patients, helps stroke survivors regain speech by engaging undamaged brain regions, and unlocks memories in people with dementia. Music stimulates dopamine release and synchronizes brainwaves among people playing together. Active music-making engages more brain regions than passive listening and produces stronger therapeutic effects. The field is evidence-based and practiced by certified therapists in hospitals, schools, and rehabilitation centers."},
"health-24":{title:"What Are Calories?",source:"Nutrition science",text:"A food calorie is actually a kilocalorie: the energy needed to raise one kilogram of water by one degree Celsius. Your body uses calories for everything from breathing to thinking. Average daily needs are about 2,000 to 2,500 for adults, varying with age, sex, and activity. Excess calories are stored as fat. A deficit forces the body to burn stored energy. However, not all calories are equal. Protein increases satiety more than sugar or refined carbs. Whole foods require more energy to digest than processed ones. This is why diet quality matters at least as much as calorie counting for weight management."},
"health-25":{title:"The Importance of Posture",source:"Orthopedic research",text:"Slouching compresses the rib cage, reducing lung capacity by up to 30 percent. For every inch your head tilts forward, the effective weight on your neck increases by about 10 pounds. Over years, this causes chronic strain. The key to good posture is awareness, core strength, and regular movement breaks. It is not about holding a rigid position but maintaining the spine's natural curves with minimal effort."},

// ===== 生活 =====
life: {title:"The Power of a Morning Routine",source:"Original",text:"How you start your morning sets the tone for the entire day. A morning routine provides structure and a sense of agency before external demands take over. Research in behavioral psychology shows that making proactive choices early builds momentum. Small wins, like making your bed or drinking a glass of water, create a cascade of positive decisions. The most effective routines are personal and sustainable. Tim Ferriss, who interviewed hundreds of high performers, found that most have some form of morning ritual, but no two are identical. The common element is not the specific activity but the intentionality: starting the day actively rather than reactively. Begin with one small habit. After a week, add another. The goal is not to optimize every minute but to create a foundation of calm and focus before the chaos of the day begins."},
"life-2":{title:"The Art of Deep Reading",source:"Maryanne Wolf, UCLA",text:"Deep reading is qualitatively different from the quick scanning we do online. It is the sustained, undistracted engagement with a text. When you read deeply, your brain constructs a mental model of the content, connecting it to prior knowledge and personal experience. This process builds critical thinking, empathy, and the capacity for sustained attention. Research by Maryanne Wolf suggests that reading on screens encourages skimming, while paper reading promotes deeper comprehension. To cultivate deep reading, set aside 30 uninterrupted minutes. Put your phone in another room. Choose material that challenges you slightly. Read at a pace that allows you to hear the words in your mind. This practice is increasingly countercultural, and for that very reason, increasingly valuable."},
"life-3":{title:"How to Build a New Habit",source:"BJ Fogg, Stanford",text:"The most common mistake in habit formation is starting too big. BJ Fogg's research shows that tiny habits, behaviors so small they require almost no motivation, are far more likely to stick. Want to start flossing? Begin with one tooth. Want to read more? Read one paragraph. The key is attaching the new habit to an existing routine. After I brush my teeth, I will floss one tooth. The existing habit serves as a trigger. Consistency matters infinitely more than intensity. A tiny habit done daily rewires the brain more effectively than a grand effort done sporadically. Once the small habit is automatic, you can expand it gradually. This method works because it bypasses the brain's resistance to change. A goal of running a marathon is intimidating. A goal of putting on your running shoes is achievable."},
"life-4":{title:"The Psychology of Procrastination",source:"Psychological research",text:"Procrastination is not a time management problem. It is an emotion regulation problem. We avoid tasks because they make us feel anxious, bored, or overwhelmed. The avoidance provides temporary relief but creates more stress in the long run. The antidote is not better scheduling. It is reducing the emotional barrier. Break the task into the smallest possible first step. Write one sentence. Open one document. The Pomodoro technique, working in focused 25-minute blocks with short breaks, helps because it makes the commitment feel manageable. Self-compassion also matters. Berating yourself for procrastinating makes it worse. Acknowledge the avoidance, forgive yourself, and take one small action."},
"life-5":{title:"The Benefits of Keeping a Journal",source:"Psychological research",text:"Regular journaling has measurable psychological and even physical benefits. Studies show it reduces stress, improves mood, and strengthens immune function. Writing about experiences helps you process them, transforming diffuse anxiety into concrete words that can be examined and reframed. Journaling also reveals patterns. Over weeks and months, you may notice recurring themes, triggers, and sources of joy that were invisible day to day. There is no single correct method. Some write pages every morning. Others record three gratitudes before bed. The only requirement is honesty. Your journal is for you alone. Write what you actually think and feel, not what you believe you should."},
"life-6":{title:"Why Sleep Matters for Students",source:"CDC",text:"Students often sacrifice sleep to study more, but this is counterproductive. Sleep is when the brain consolidates memories, transferring information from short-term to long-term storage. A student who sleeps well after studying retains significantly more than one who stays awake. Sleep deprivation also impairs attention, problem-solving, and emotional regulation, making learning less efficient. The night before an exam, it is better to sleep well than to stay up cramming. If you must choose between an extra hour of study and an extra hour of sleep, choose sleep."},
"life-7":{title:"How to Give a Great Presentation",source:"Communication research",text:"Public speaking anxiety is common, but effective presenting is a learnable skill. Start by understanding your audience. What do they already know? What do they care about? Structure your talk around three main points. People rarely remember more. Use stories and concrete examples. A vivid story lingers in memory long after a statistic fades. Practice out loud, ideally in front of someone. Record yourself. You will notice filler words and habits you were unaware of. When presenting, remember the audience wants you to succeed. Speak slightly slower than feels natural. Pause between points. Make eye contact with individuals rather than scanning the whole room."},
"life-8":{title:"The Art of Asking Good Questions",source:"Educational psychology",text:"Asking good questions is a superpower. Good questions demonstrate genuine interest, deepen understanding, and make conversations more meaningful. Open questions, beginning with what, how, or why, invite the other person to share. Closed questions, answerable with yes or no, are useful for confirming facts but do not encourage elaboration. The most powerful questions are often the simplest: What was that like? How did you decide? What do you make of that? Follow-up questions are especially valuable because they signal that you were truly listening, not just waiting for your turn to speak. In a world of distracted half-attention, a good questioner stands out."},
"life-9":{title:"Minimalism: Less Is More",source:"Lifestyle research",text:"Minimalism is not about owning as little as possible. It is about being intentional with what you own. Physical clutter competes for your attention and creates low-grade stress. Research suggests that people with cluttered homes have higher cortisol levels. Decluttering frees mental bandwidth. The practice is simple: before acquiring something new, ask whether it will genuinely add value to your life. Apply the same principle to commitments and digital consumption. Minimalism is not deprivation. It is making space, literally and psychologically, for what matters most."},
"life-10":{title:"How to Manage Your Time Better",source:"Productivity research",text:"Time management is not about cramming more into your day. It is about ensuring the most important things get done. Identify your two or three highest-priority tasks and do them first, before checking messages or social media. This is sometimes called eating the frog. Time blocking, scheduling specific tasks for specific hours, works better than open-ended to-do lists. Learn to say no. Every yes to something unimportant is a no to something that matters. Finally, protect your peak energy hours. Most people have a window of 2 to 3 hours when they are most cognitively sharp. Guard that time for your most important work."},
"life-11":{title:"The Importance of Hobbies",source:"Psychology research",text:"Hobbies are not frivolous. They are essential for well-being. Engaging in an activity purely for enjoyment produces a state psychologists call flow: complete absorption where time seems to disappear. Flow states reduce stress and increase happiness. Hobbies also provide a sense of progress and mastery independent of work or academic achievement. They connect you with communities of like-minded people. The specific hobby matters less than the engagement. Gardening, painting, playing an instrument, hiking, cooking: the common thread is doing something for its own sake, not for external reward."},
"life-12":{title:"How to Write a Good Email",source:"Professional communication",text:"Good emails respect the reader's time. State your purpose in the first sentence. Use specific subject lines. Meeting Thursday is vague. Requesting agenda items for Thursday's budget review is clear. Make the desired action obvious. If you need a reply by Friday, say so explicitly. Keep it brief. If an email runs longer than a few paragraphs, consider whether a phone call or meeting would be more effective. Proofread before sending. Typos signal carelessness. Finally, never send an email when you are angry. Write it, save it as a draft, and review it the next morning."},
"life-13":{title:"Learning a Language: Where to Start",source:"Language acquisition research",text:"The key to language learning is comprehensible input: reading and listening to material slightly above your current level. You should understand most of it, with a few new elements to absorb from context. Grammar study and vocabulary drilling are supplements, not the main event. Consistency matters more than intensity. Thirty minutes daily beats three hours once a week. Find content you genuinely enjoy in the target language. When learning feels like entertainment rather than study, motivation becomes self-sustaining. Speaking and writing are important but work best after building a foundation through extensive listening and reading."},
"life-14":{title:"How to Handle Criticism Well",source:"Psychology research",text:"Receiving criticism triggers a threat response. Your first instinct may be to defend yourself. Pause. Take a breath. Try to separate the message from its delivery. Even poorly expressed criticism may contain useful information. Ask clarifying questions before responding. Distinguish between constructive feedback, which is meant to help you improve, and destructive criticism, which is meant to wound. Consider the source. Not all opinions carry equal weight. The ability to hear hard truths without crumbling is one of the most valuable skills you can develop. It accelerates growth in every domain."},
"life-15":{title:"The Value of Travel",source:"Cultural psychology",text:"Travel makes your own culture's assumptions visible. People who have lived abroad score higher on creativity and cognitive flexibility. Travel builds empathy by replacing stereotypes with real human encounters. You do not need to go far. Visiting a different neighborhood or trying unfamiliar food exercises the same mental muscles. The key is curiosity and openness to experiences outside your comfort zone."},
"life-16":{title:"The Five-Minute Rule",source:"Productivity research",text:"Starting is always the hardest part. The five-minute rule exploits a psychological quirk: commit to doing a dreaded task for just five minutes, and you are allowed to stop afterward. Most of the time, once begun, the task is not as bad as anticipated and you continue. The rule works because anticipatory anxiety is usually worse than the experience itself. The commitment must be genuinely small and achievable. Tell yourself you will write one sentence. Put on your running shoes. Open one textbook. The barrier to starting drops to nearly zero."},
"life-17":{title:"The Art of Listening",source:"Communication studies",text:"Most people do not listen to understand. They listen to reply. Active listening means giving full attention to the speaker, not formulating your response while they talk. It involves three components: paying attention, showing you are listening through eye contact and nodding, and responding thoughtfully with questions or brief summaries. Good listeners make others feel heard and valued. In a world competing for attention, being a genuine listener is rare and powerful. People remember how you made them feel more than what you said."},
"life-18":{title:"How to Apologize Sincerely",source:"Psychology research",text:"A good apology has four elements: acknowledge specifically what you did wrong, express genuine regret, offer to make amends, and explain how you will prevent it from happening again. Avoid I am sorry you feel that way, which deflects responsibility. Do not include excuses or justifications. Sincere apologies restore trust and can strengthen relationships. Research shows people who apologize are perceived as more competent, not less. Admitting fault takes courage. People respect those who can do it."},
"life-19":{title:"Why Multitasking Does Not Work",source:"Cognitive neuroscience",text:"The brain cannot focus on two cognitive tasks simultaneously. What we call multitasking is actually rapid task-switching. Each switch incurs a cost in time and accuracy. Studies show heavy multitaskers perform worse on attention and memory tests than those who focus on one thing at a time. For tasks requiring thought, single-tasking is far more efficient. Turn off notifications. Close extra tabs. Give one task your undivided attention."},
"life-20":{title:"How to Read Faster Without Losing Comprehension",source:"Cognitive psychology",text:"True speed reading, absorbing thousands of words per minute with full comprehension, is not biologically possible. The eye can only focus on a narrow area at a time. However, you can read more efficiently. Reduce subvocalization, the habit of silently pronouncing each word. Preview material before diving in: scan headings, first sentences, and summaries to give your brain a framework. Adjust your reading speed to the material's difficulty. Skim familiar content, slow down for complex passages."},
"life-21":{title:"The Power of a Growth Mindset",source:"Carol Dweck, Stanford",text:"Psychologist Carol Dweck identified two fundamental mindsets. A fixed mindset believes intelligence is static. A growth mindset believes abilities can be developed. These beliefs become self-fulfilling. Students taught that the brain can grow like a muscle earn higher grades and persist longer after failure. The good news is that mindsets are malleable. Simply learning about neuroplasticity, the brain's ability to form new connections throughout life, can shift how you approach challenges. Praise effort and strategy, not innate talent."},
"life-22":{title:"How to Network Without Feeling Fake",source:"Career development research",text:"Genuine networking is about building relationships, not collecting contacts. The best networkers give more than they take. They share knowledge, make introductions, and offer help without keeping score. Start with people you already know. Be curious about their work. When meeting someone new, focus on understanding what they do and why they find it meaningful. A thoughtful follow-up message after a conversation is more valuable than a stack of business cards. Authentic connections develop over time, not from a single interaction."},
"life-23":{title:"The Benefits of Learning an Instrument",source:"Neuroscience research",text:"Playing music engages nearly every brain region simultaneously and strengthens connections between hemispheres. Musicians have measurably thicker corpus callosums, the bridge between left and right brains. Children who study music tend to perform better academically. Adults who play have slower age-related cognitive decline. The benefits come from consistent practice, not innate talent. Even a few months of regular playing produces measurable brain changes. Music also provides a lifelong outlet for emotional expression and social connection."},
"life-24":{title:"How to Argue Better",source:"Conflict resolution research",text:"Most arguments aim to win, not to understand. Constructive disagreement is a skill. Start by listening to the other person's position and repeating it back in your own words before responding. Use I statements: I feel frustrated when undermines defensiveness less than You always. Avoid absolutes like always and never. Focus on the specific issue, not the person's character. When emotions escalate, take a break. The prefrontal cortex, responsible for rational thought, goes offline during intense anger. A 20-minute pause resets the conversation. The goal is understanding, not victory."},
"life-25":{title:"The Value of Volunteering",source:"Social psychology",text:"Volunteering benefits the volunteer as much as the recipient. Studies link regular volunteering with lower depression rates, better physical health, and greater life satisfaction. Helping others activates reward circuits in the brain and provides a sense of purpose. Volunteering also builds skills and social connections. The type of volunteering matters less than consistency. A few hours monthly produces measurable benefits. Start with something aligned with your interests and values. The commitment should feel sustainable, not burdensome."},

// ===== 文化 =====
culture: {title:"The History of the Handshake",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The handshake originated in ancient Greece as a gesture of peace. Extending an open right hand showed you carried no weapon. The Romans grasped forearms instead, checking for hidden daggers. Over centuries, the handshake evolved into a symbol of trust and agreement used worldwide. However, customs vary. In Japan, bowing is the standard greeting. In Thailand, the wai, a slight bow with pressed palms, is preferred. The pandemic prompted a reevaluation of handshakes, with elbow bumps and nods gaining acceptance. The gesture's 2,500-year history shows how a simple physical act can carry deep cultural meaning."},
"culture-2":{title:"The Origins of Tea Culture",source:"Wikipedia",text:"According to legend, tea was discovered around 2737 BCE when leaves blew into Chinese Emperor Shen Nung's boiling water. From China, tea spread to Japan, where the tea ceremony became a meditative practice emphasizing harmony, respect, and tranquility. Portuguese traders brought tea to Europe in the 16th century. It became wildly popular in Britain, where afternoon tea was formalized in the 1840s. The British addiction to tea reshaped global trade, leading to tea plantations across India and Sri Lanka. Today, distinct tea cultures flourish worldwide: mint tea in Morocco, chai in India, bubble tea in Taiwan, sweet iced tea in the American South."},
"culture-3":{title:"Why Do We Have Birthdays?",source:"Cultural history",text:"The ancient Egyptians celebrated the pharaoh's coronation as his divine birthday. The Greeks added candles to cakes, honoring Artemis with glowing offerings. Birthday parties for ordinary people only became common in the 19th century with the rise of the middle class. The song Happy Birthday to You, originally Good Morning to All, was written in 1893 by two American sisters and is now the most recognized song in English. Traditions vary: Mexicans strike piñatas, Koreans eat seaweed soup, and in many African cultures, elaborate birthday celebrations are a relatively recent adoption."},
"culture-4":{title:"The Story of Chocolate",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Chocolate began as a bitter drink. The Olmecs, Mayans, and Aztecs of ancient Mesoamerica used cacao beans as currency and consumed chocolate during religious rituals. Spanish explorers brought cacao to Europe in the 16th century, adding sugar to sweeten it. The first solid chocolate bar was created in 1847 in England. Milk chocolate was invented in Switzerland in 1875. Today, the global chocolate industry is worth over 100 billion dollars annually. Yet many cacao farmers earn less than a dollar a day, and the industry faces ongoing challenges with child labor and deforestation."},
"culture-5":{title:"The Olympic Games",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The ancient Olympics began in 776 BCE in Olympia, Greece. Athletes competed in running, wrestling, and chariot racing during a religious festival honoring Zeus. The Games continued for over a millennium before being banned in 393 CE by a Christian emperor who considered them pagan. They were revived in 1896 by French educator Pierre de Coubertin, who believed international sports could promote peace. The Olympic rings represent the five inhabited continents. The flame, carried by relay from Olympia, symbolizes the link between ancient and modern Games. Today, the Olympics are the world's largest sporting event, watched by billions."},
"culture-6":{title:"The Power of Music Across Cultures",source:"Anthropology",text:"Music exists in every human culture studied. Archaeologists have found bone flutes over 40,000 years old, suggesting music predates agriculture and permanent settlements. Across cultures, music serves similar functions: strengthening community bonds, expressing emotions, and accompanying rituals. Certain patterns recur universally. Most cultures use octaves. Major keys feel happy and minor keys sad, likely reflecting the acoustic properties of the human voice. Music releases dopamine in the brain. Playing music together synchronizes performers' brainwaves, creating a profound sense of connection."},
"culture-7":{title:"Why We Tell Stories",source:"Anthropology",text:"Storytelling is older than writing. Before writing, oral stories preserved history, taught moral lessons, and explained the natural world. The oldest known narrative, the Epic of Gilgamesh, was written in Mesopotamia over 4,000 years ago. Most stories follow a pattern Joseph Campbell called the hero's journey: a protagonist leaves home, faces trials, gains wisdom, and returns changed. This structure appears in myths, novels, and films from every continent. Stories remain central to human life. We organize our memories as narratives. The news we consume, the entertainment we enjoy, and the way we understand ourselves are all structured as stories."},
"culture-8":{title:"The History of Money",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Before money, people bartered directly. A farmer traded grain for shoes, but both parties had to want what the other offered, a frustrating limitation. The first money appeared around 5,000 years ago in Mesopotamia, where silver bars served as a medium of exchange. China introduced paper money in the 7th century. The word salary derives from sal, Latin for salt, because Roman soldiers were sometimes paid in salt. Today, most money exists only as electronic records. Cryptocurrencies represent the latest evolution: digital money not controlled by any government."},
"culture-9":{title:"The Art of Bonsai",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Bonsai is the Japanese art of cultivating miniature trees. The practice originated in China over a thousand years ago as penjing and was refined in Japan. Bonsai trees are not genetic dwarfs. They are kept small through careful pruning, wiring, and root restriction. A well-maintained bonsai can live for centuries, passed down through generations. Bonsai is more than horticulture. It is a form of meditation and artistic expression. Each tree embodies the Japanese aesthetic principles of simplicity, asymmetry, and the beauty of age."},
"culture-10":{title:"How Coffee Changed the World",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Coffee was discovered in Ethiopia, where legend says a goat herder noticed his flock became energetic after eating coffee berries. By the 15th century, coffee houses had become vital social institutions in the Middle East. In 17th-century Europe, coffee houses were called penny universities because for the price of a cup, you could participate in intellectual discussions. Coffee fueled the Enlightenment and the Industrial Revolution. Today, it is one of the world's most traded commodities, supporting over 100 million livelihoods. Climate change threatens coffee-growing regions, and the industry grapples with fair trade and sustainability challenges."},
"culture-11":{title:"The Golden Rule Around the World",source:"Comparative religion",text:"The principle of treating others as you wish to be treated appears in nearly every ethical tradition. Confucius stated it negatively: Do not impose on others what you do not desire for yourself. Jesus taught the positive form: Do unto others as you would have them do unto you. Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, and Judaism all contain similar formulations. The independent emergence of this idea across cultures suggests it reflects a universal human intuition about fairness and reciprocity, the foundation of moral behavior."},
"culture-12":{title:"Body Language Across Cultures",source:"Cross-cultural studies",text:"The same gesture can communicate radically different meanings across cultures. A thumbs-up signals approval in most Western countries but is offensive in parts of the Middle East and West Africa. Direct eye contact conveys confidence in the West but can be disrespectful in East Asia. Personal space norms vary. Latin Americans tend to stand closer during conversation than Northern Europeans. Understanding these differences is increasingly essential in a globalized world. What is polite in one culture may be hostile in another."},
"culture-13":{title:"The Evolution of Fashion",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Clothing began for warmth, but quickly became about status. In ancient Rome, only the emperor could wear purple. Fashion as rapid, seasonal change emerged in 14th-century Europe with the rise of trade. The sewing machine, invented in the 19th century, democratized fashionable clothing. The 20th century saw fashion become a global industry. Today, fast fashion brands release new styles weekly, raising urgent concerns about environmental sustainability and labor conditions. The average garment is now worn only seven times before disposal."},
"culture-14":{title:"Why We Celebrate New Year",source:"Wikipedia",text:"New Year celebrations are among humanity's oldest traditions. The Babylonians celebrated the new year 4,000 years ago with an 11-day festival. Julius Caesar established January 1st as the Roman New Year, naming the month after Janus, the two-faced god who looked backward and forward. Different cultures mark different dates. Chinese New Year falls in late January or February. Rosh Hashanah occurs in September or October. The Islamic New Year shifts through the seasons. Common themes unite these diverse celebrations: reflecting on the past, resolving to improve, and gathering with loved ones."},
"culture-15":{title:"The Rise of Social Media",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The first social network, Six Degrees, launched in 1997. Facebook, started from a Harvard dorm in 2004, now connects billions. Social media has democratized information and given voice to marginalized groups. But it has also created echo chambers, spread misinformation, and raised concerns about privacy and adolescent mental health. Society is still learning how to harness its benefits while mitigating harms."},
"culture-16":{title:"The Dead Sea Scrolls",source:"Wikipedia",text:"In 1947, a shepherd searching for a lost goat discovered clay jars in a cave near the Dead Sea. Inside were ancient scrolls. Over the next decade, thousands of fragments were recovered from 11 caves. The scrolls include the oldest known copies of the Hebrew Bible, dating back over 2,000 years, along with previously unknown religious texts. They were likely hidden by a Jewish sect called the Essenes during a time of conflict. The scrolls transformed our understanding of the Bible's development and the diversity of Jewish thought during the time of Jesus."},
"culture-17":{title:"The Origins of Halloween",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Halloween blends Celtic and Christian traditions. The Celtic festival of Samhain, celebrated on October 31st, marked the night when the boundary between living and dead was believed to blur. People wore costumes to ward off spirits. The Christian church later designated November 1st as All Saints Day. The evening before became All Hallows Eve. Irish immigrants brought Halloween to America in the 19th century. Trick-or-treating evolved from the medieval practice of going door to door offering prayers for the dead in exchange for food."},
"culture-18":{title:"The Great Wall of China",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The Great Wall is not a single wall but a series of fortifications built over nearly 2,000 years by different dynasties. The earliest sections date to the 7th century BCE. The most famous parts were built during the Ming Dynasty. The total length exceeds 21,000 kilometers, though contrary to popular belief, it cannot be seen from space with the naked eye. Millions of workers, including soldiers and prisoners, labored on it over centuries. Many died and were buried within it. Today, it is a UNESCO World Heritage site and an enduring symbol of Chinese civilization."},
"culture-19":{title:"Why We Love Hero Stories",source:"Comparative mythology",text:"Joseph Campbell identified a universal narrative pattern he called the hero's journey: a protagonist leaves home, faces trials with the help of mentors, confronts evil, and returns transformed. This structure appears in the stories of Buddha, Jesus, Harry Potter, and Luke Skywalker. Psychologists suggest it mirrors the arc of human development. We all leave the safety of childhood, face challenges in the adult world, and ideally return with wisdom. Hero stories inspire because they show that ordinary people can rise to extraordinary challenges."},
"culture-20":{title:"The History of Zero",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Zero as a number was a revolutionary concept. Ancient civilizations had symbols for placeholder zeros but did not treat them as numbers. The full concept of zero emerged in India around the 5th century CE. The mathematician Brahmagupta wrote the first rules for arithmetic with zero. From India, the concept spread to the Islamic world and then Europe. The Arabic word sifr became both cipher and zero. The invention of zero enabled algebra, calculus, and eventually the binary code that powers all digital computers."},
"culture-21":{title:"The Silk Road",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of trade routes connecting China to the Mediterranean for over 1,500 years. Silk was just one commodity. Spices, glass, precious stones, and religious ideas all traveled these routes. Buddhism spread from India to China. Islam entered Central Asia. Few traders traveled the entire length. Goods passed through many hands, increasing in price at each stop. The routes declined as sea travel became cheaper and safer after the 15th century."},
"culture-22":{title:"How Chess Conquered the World",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Chess originated in 6th-century India as chaturanga, representing an army's four divisions. It spread to Persia and then Europe via Arab conquests. The modern game took shape in 15th-century Europe when the queen and bishop gained their powerful moves. The first World Championship was held in 1886. In 1997, IBM's Deep Blue defeated champion Garry Kasparov, marking a milestone in artificial intelligence. Today, online platforms enable anyone to play opponents worldwide at any time."},
"culture-23":{title:"The Mystery of Stonehenge",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Stonehenge consists of massive stones arranged in concentric circles on Salisbury Plain, England. The largest stones weigh over 25 tons and were transported from up to 250 kilometers away. Built in stages over 1,500 years starting around 3000 BCE, its purpose remains debated. The most accepted theory is that it was a ceremonial site aligned with the sun. On the summer solstice, the sun rises directly over the Heel Stone and shines into the monument's center. Cremated remains suggest it may also have been a burial ground."},
"culture-24":{title:"The Eiffel Tower: From Eyesore to Icon",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Built for the 1889 World's Fair, the Eiffel Tower was meant to be temporary. Many Parisians hated it. A group of prominent artists called it a monstrous eyesore. It was saved from demolition because its height made it ideal for radio transmission. Its antennas intercepted enemy communications during World War I. Today, it is the most visited paid monument in the world, welcoming nearly 7 million visitors annually. At 330 meters, it was the tallest structure in the world until 1930. The iron expands and contracts with temperature, making the tower about 15 centimeters taller in summer."},
"culture-25":{title:"The Invention of Paper",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Paper was invented in China around 105 CE by Cai Lun, a court official who used tree bark, hemp, rags, and fishing nets. The materials were soaked, beaten into pulp, and pressed into thin sheets. The technology spread to Korea and Japan in the 7th century, the Islamic world in the 8th, and Europe in the 12th. The printing press dramatically increased demand. Today, the global paper industry produces over 400 million tons annually, even as digital media reduces demand for printing and writing paper."},

// ===== 自然 =====
nature: {title:"The Secret Life of Bees",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A single honeybee hive contains up to 60,000 bees living in an extraordinarily organized society. The queen is the mother of almost the entire colony and can lay up to 2,000 eggs per day. Worker bees, all female, cycle through a series of careers during their short six-week lives: cleaning cells, feeding larvae, building honeycomb, guarding the entrance, and finally foraging for nectar and pollen. A single worker produces only about one-twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her lifetime. Bees communicate through the famous waggle dance. A forager returning with news of a rich nectar source performs a figure-eight movement whose angle relative to vertical indicates the direction relative to the sun, and whose duration signals the distance. Bees pollinate roughly one-third of the world's food crops, making them indispensable to agriculture and ecosystems."},
"nature-2":{title:"Coral Reefs: Rainforests of the Sea",source:"NOAA",text:"Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor but support roughly 25 percent of all marine species. Corals are colonies of tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp secretes a calcium carbonate skeleton. Inside their tissues live microscopic algae that photosynthesize, providing the coral with up to 90 percent of its energy. In return, the coral provides the algae with shelter. This partnership allows coral reefs to thrive in clear, nutrient-poor tropical waters. When water temperatures rise just one or two degrees above normal, corals expel their algae and turn white, a phenomenon called bleaching. Bleached corals are not dead, but they are starving. If temperatures return to normal quickly, they can recover. If not, they die. Scientists estimate half of the world's coral reefs have been lost in the past 30 years to climate change, pollution, and overfishing."},
"nature-3":{title:"The Water Cycle",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Water on Earth has been continuously recycled for billions of years. The sun evaporates water from oceans, lakes, and soil. Plants release water vapor through transpiration. This vapor rises, cools, and condenses into clouds. When droplets become heavy enough, they fall as rain or snow. Some water soaks into the ground, replenishing aquifers. The rest flows through rivers back to the oceans. The water molecules in your glass today might have once quenched a dinosaur's thirst, fallen as snow on a prehistoric mountain, or drifted as a cloud over an ancient ocean. The total amount of water on Earth is essentially fixed. It just moves, in an endless global cycle that sustains all terrestrial life."},
"nature-4":{title:"The Monarch Butterfly Migration",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Each fall, millions of monarch butterflies travel up to 4,000 kilometers from Canada and the United States to the mountains of central Mexico. No single butterfly completes the round trip. The butterflies that arrive in Mexico are the great-grandchildren of those that left the previous spring. Yet they find the exact same forests where their ancestors wintered. How they navigate remains partially mysterious. Scientists believe monarchs use a combination of the sun's position and the Earth's magnetic field. The migration is threatened by the loss of milkweed, the only plant monarch caterpillars eat, due to agricultural herbicides. Conservation efforts now focus on planting milkweed corridors along the migration route."},
"nature-5":{title:"How Do Volcanoes Form?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Volcanoes form where molten rock, called magma, rises through the Earth's crust. Most volcanoes occur at plate boundaries. The Pacific Ring of Fire contains about 75 percent of the world's active volcanoes. When two plates collide, one slides beneath the other and melts, creating magma that rises to the surface. Some volcanoes erupt explosively, like Mount St. Helens in 1980. Others produce gentle lava flows, like those on Hawaii, which was built entirely by volcanic activity. Volcanic soil is extraordinarily fertile. Millions of people live on the slopes of active volcanoes because the land yields abundant crops."},
"nature-6":{title:"The Deep Ocean",source:"NOAA",text:"More than 80 percent of the ocean remains unexplored and unmapped. The deep sea is the largest habitat on Earth, yet we know less about it than the surface of Mars. In the deepest trenches, pressure exceeds a thousand atmospheres and sunlight never penetrates. Yet life thrives. Deep-sea creatures have evolved remarkable adaptations: bioluminescence for communication and hunting, enormous mouths and expandable stomachs for capturing rare prey, and the ability to survive on the organic snow that drifts down from sunlit waters above. Hydrothermal vents spew superheated, mineral-rich water, supporting ecosystems powered not by sunlight but by chemosynthesis, a discovery that revolutionized our understanding of where life can exist."},
"nature-7":{title:"How Plants Communicate",source:"Plant biology research",text:"Plants seem silent, but they communicate constantly. When attacked by insects, they release volatile chemicals that warn neighboring plants, which then preemptively produce defensive compounds. Below ground, most plants are connected by networks of fungi that scientists call the wood wide web. Trees share water, nutrients, and even warning signals through these fungal connections. A mother tree can send carbon to its shaded seedlings. Research suggests plants can learn, remember, and make decisions, though their intelligence operates on timescales far slower than ours."},
"nature-8":{title:"The Northern Lights",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The aurora borealis appears as curtains of green, pink, and purple light dancing across polar skies. It is caused by charged particles from the sun colliding with atoms in Earth's upper atmosphere. The particles are funneled toward the poles by Earth's magnetic field. Oxygen produces green and red light. Nitrogen produces blue and purple. The best viewing locations are northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Canada, and Alaska. The lights are most active during solar storms, which follow an 11-year cycle. The next solar maximum peaks around 2025, offering prime viewing opportunities."},
"nature-9":{title:"The Incredible Octopus",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Octopuses are among the most intelligent invertebrates. They have large brains, excellent eyesight, and can solve complex puzzles, open jars, and recognize individual humans. Their skin contains specialized cells that change color, pattern, and texture in a fraction of a second for camouflage or communication. An octopus has three hearts and blue blood. Its blood uses copper-based hemocyanin to transport oxygen, more efficient than iron-based hemoglobin in cold, low-oxygen water. Most species live only one to two years. The female lays tens of thousands of eggs and guards them without eating for months until they hatch, after which she dies."},
"nature-10":{title:"Why Leaves Change Color",source:"Wikipedia",text:"During spring and summer, leaves are green because they are packed with chlorophyll, the pigment that captures sunlight for photosynthesis. As autumn approaches and days shorten, trees stop producing chlorophyll. The existing chlorophyll breaks down, revealing yellow and orange carotenoids that were present all along. Red and purple anthocyanins are produced specifically in autumn, possibly to protect leaves from sun damage while the tree reabsorbs nutrients. The most vibrant colors occur when sunny, cool days follow cold nights. Once nutrients are recovered, the leaves detach and fall."},
"nature-11":{title:"The Amazon Rainforest",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The Amazon is the largest tropical rainforest on Earth, spanning nine South American countries. One in ten known species lives there. Its trees absorb billions of tons of carbon dioxide and produce about 20 percent of the world's oxygen. Deforestation for cattle ranching and soy farming has destroyed roughly 17 percent of the forest. Scientists warn that if deforestation continues beyond a tipping point, the Amazon could transition from rainforest to savanna, releasing vast amounts of carbon and accelerating climate change. Protecting the Amazon is one of the most urgent environmental challenges of our time."},
"nature-12":{title:"How Birds Navigate Migration",source:"Ornithology research",text:"Billions of birds migrate annually between breeding and wintering grounds. The Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to Antarctica and back each year, a 70,000-kilometer round trip. Birds navigate using multiple systems: they sense Earth's magnetic field through specialized cells, track the sun and stars, and recognize landmarks like coastlines. Young birds on their first migration rely more on instinct, while experienced birds use memory. Some species can even perceive polarized light patterns invisible to humans."},
"nature-13":{title:"What Is Lightning?",source:"Wikipedia",text:"A lightning bolt is a massive electrical discharge. The air inside a bolt heats to roughly 30,000 degrees Celsius, five times hotter than the sun's surface, causing it to expand explosively as thunder. Lightning forms when ice particles collide in storm clouds, separating electrical charges. When the charge difference between cloud and ground becomes large enough, a conductive channel opens and the discharge occurs. Light travels faster than sound, so count the seconds between flash and thunder. Every five seconds equals roughly one mile of distance."},
"nature-14":{title:"The Secret World of Fungi",source:"Mycology research",text:"Fungi form their own kingdom of life, neither plant nor animal. A single fungus in Oregon spans nearly ten square kilometers underground and may be thousands of years old, making it one of Earth's largest and oldest organisms. Fungi decompose dead matter, recycle nutrients, and form partnerships with most plant roots, helping them absorb water and minerals. Without fungi, forests as we know them could not exist. They have also given us penicillin, the first antibiotic, and are essential for making bread, cheese, beer, and wine."},
"nature-15":{title:"How Glaciers Shape Landscapes",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Glaciers are rivers of ice that flow slowly across land. They form where snowfall exceeds melt, compressing into solid ice over decades. Glaciers cover 10 percent of Earth's land and hold about 69 percent of the planet's fresh water. As they move, they carve valleys, grind mountains, and transport boulders over vast distances. Many of the world's most dramatic landscapes were sculpted by glaciers during the last ice age. Most glaciers are now shrinking due to climate change, contributing to sea level rise and threatening water supplies for millions."},
"nature-16":{title:"A Worker Bee's Life",source:"Wikipedia",text:"During summer, a worker honeybee lives about six weeks. For the first three days she cleans cells. Then she becomes a nurse, feeding larvae. At about a week, she produces wax and builds comb. Later, she guards the hive entrance. Finally, at three weeks, she becomes a forager, flying up to five kilometers to collect nectar and pollen. She makes roughly ten trips per day."},
"nature-17":{title:"The Fastest Animals",source:"Wikipedia",text:"The peregrine falcon dives at over 320 kilometers per hour, making it Earth's fastest animal. The cheetah, the fastest land animal, reaches 120 kilometers per hour in short bursts. In water, the sailfish swims at 110 kilometers per hour. Each of these animals is a marvel of evolutionary engineering, with specialized adaptations for speed."},
"nature-18":{title:"Why the Ocean Is Salty",source:"NOAA",text:"Rain erodes rocks, dissolving tiny amounts of minerals that rivers carry to the ocean. Sodium and chloride make up about 85 percent of seawater salt. Over millions of years, water evaporates but salt remains, making the ocean increasingly salty. If all the salt were spread over land, it would form a 150-meter-thick layer."},
"nature-19":{title:"How Seeds Travel",source:"Botany research",text:"Plants cannot move, but their seeds travel remarkable distances. Dandelion seeds have parachutes that ride the wind. Coconuts float across oceans. Burdock seeds hitchhike on animal fur. Squirrels bury acorns, accidentally planting oak trees. Each strategy represents an evolutionary solution to the problem of dispersal."},
"nature-20":{title:"Dolphin Communication",source:"Marine biology",text:"Dolphins use a sophisticated system of clicks and whistles. Each individual has a unique signature whistle, essentially a name that other dolphins use to address them. They also use echolocation for navigation and hunting, producing clicks that bounce off objects, revealing their size, shape, and distance. Dolphins live in cooperative social pods and display behaviors suggesting high intelligence."},
"nature-21":{title:"The Secret of Gecko Feet",source:"Biomimetics research",text:"Geckos walk on walls and ceilings thanks to millions of microscopic hairs on their toe pads called setae. These split into hundreds of even smaller structures that interact with surfaces through van der Waals forces, weak molecular attractions. Across billions of contact points, these forces create powerful adhesion. A gecko can hang from a single toe. The feet are self-cleaning and never leave residue."},
"nature-22":{title:"Desert Animal Survival",source:"Desert ecology",text:"The fennec fox's enormous ears radiate heat. The kangaroo rat never drinks water, extracting all moisture from seeds. Camels store fat, not water, in their humps, metabolizing it when food is scarce. Many desert animals are nocturnal to avoid extreme daytime heat. Each adaptation represents an elegant solution to one of Earth's harshest environments."},
"nature-23":{title:"Why Leaves Are Green",source:"Wikipedia",text:"Leaves contain chlorophyll, a pigment that absorbs red and blue light to power photosynthesis while reflecting green. This is why plants appear green. In autumn, as chlorophyll breaks down, underlying yellow and orange carotenoids emerge, creating fall colors. The specific green of chlorophyll may have evolved because it absorbs the most energetic parts of the spectrum while avoiding damage from excess energy."},
"nature-24":{title:"The Journey of a Raindrop",source:"Hydrology",text:"A water molecule spends about nine days in the atmosphere before falling as rain. On the ground, it might flow through rivers for weeks, rest in a lake for years, or remain locked in a glacier for millennia. The water you drink today circulated through dinosaurs millions of years ago. Earth has a fixed amount of water, endlessly recycled through the water cycle."},
"nature-25":{title:"The Intelligence of Crows",source:"Animal cognition",text:"Crows use tools, solve multi-step puzzles, recognize human faces, and hold grudges against specific people for years. In experiments, they have bent wires into hooks to retrieve food, a behavior once thought unique to apes. They hold what appear to be funerals, gathering around dead crows. Their brains achieve intelligence through a completely different evolutionary path from mammals, demonstrating convergent evolution."}
};
